{"qid":"Q1469100","names":["Beylik of Karaman"],"title":"Beylik of Karaman","endonym":{"native":"Karamanoğulları","lang":"tr"},"capsule":"The Beylik of Karaman (Karamanoğulları) was the most powerful of the Anatolian beyliks that arose after the Mongol defeat of the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum at Köse Dağ (1243), controlling the historic Karamanian heartland in south-central Anatolia from around 1250. At its height under Karamanoğlu Mehmed Bey in 1277, the Karamanids briefly seized Konya and proclaimed the Turkish vernacular the sole language of administration, directly challenging Mongol-backed Seljuk authority. They were the fiercest and most persistent rivals of the rising Ottoman dynasty, repeatedly allying with Ottoman enemies and recovering territory after Ottoman defeats. Mehmed II finally crushed the beylik after his capture of its capital Karaman in 1468, incorporating it permanently into the Ottoman state.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core territorial extent around the Taurus highlands is well established; frontier zones with neighboring beyliks and Ottomans fluctuated considerably and are less precisely mapped.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Karamanids","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karamanids"},{"label":"Britannica: Karamanids","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Karamanids"},{"label":"Britannica: Anatolia — Turkish beyliks","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Anatolia"}],"britannica":"place/Karaman","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Karamanids","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Bedreddin Mahmud Bey","nativeName":"بدر الدین محمود بك","roman":"Bedreddin Mahmud Bey","start